paperAbstract:
The fact that practitioners of solid-state NMR can participate in many branches of mathematics, physical sciences, and biological sciences, using similar principles and similar experimental equipment, is one of the most attractive features of this area of research. To illustrate this point, I discuss several of my own research projects, starting in the early 1980s and proceeding to the present, and ranging from radiofrequency pulse sequence design to investigations of fullerenes, semiconductor quantum wells, amyloid fibrils associated with Alzheimer's disease, and protein folding. The emphasis of the discussion is on the circumstances surrounding these projects, which may be of interest both to my contemporaries and to younger scientists. 


Keywords: 

solid-state NMR; 
composite pulses; 
zero-field NMR; 
fullerenes; 
skyrmions, quantum wells; 
protein structure; 
dipolar recoupling; 
amyloid fibrils

